# `actuarial::losses::compound_poisson`

Mean and variance of a compound Poisson aggregate loss.

## Signature

```cpp
actuarial::losses::AggregateMoments compound_poisson(double lambda, double severity_mean, double severity_variance, pluss::Platform platform = pluss::Platform::CPU);
```

## What It Does

The aggregate of a Poisson number of claims with rate {math}`\lambda` and a severity of mean {math}`\mu_X` and variance {math}`\sigma^2_X`: {math}`E[S]=\lambda\mu_X` and {math}`\mathrm{Var}[S]=\lambda(\sigma_X^2+\mu_X^2)=\lambda\,E[X^2]`. It is [`poisson_moments`](poisson_moments.md) fed into [`aggregate_moments`](aggregate_moments.md) in one call, for a single portfolio. Use it for a quick aggregate when the count is Poisson; for a more variable count use [`compound_negbin`](compound_negbin.md).

## Parameters

`lambda`
: The expected number of claims, finite and 0 or more.

`severity_mean`, `severity_variance`
: Mean and variance of one claim; finite and 0 or more.

`platform`
: Where the result is placed. Default `pluss::Platform::CPU`.

## Returns

An [`AggregateMoments`](AggregateMoments.md) of two rank-zero series.

## Errors

Throws the `poisson_moments` errors for `lambda`, and `compound_poisson: severity_mean must be >= 0`, `compound_poisson: severity_variance must be >= 0` and the `must be finite` errors.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: a portfolio

Two claims a year of mean 100 and variance 10,000.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto m = actuarial::losses::compound_poisson(2.0, 100.0, 10000.0);
  out.writeln("mean =", m.mean.item<double>(), " variance =", m.variance.item<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: variance is lambda times the second moment

The identity with E[X^2] = variance + mean^2.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  const auto m = actuarial::losses::compound_poisson(2.0, 100.0, 10000.0);
  out.writeln(m.variance.item<double>(), 2.0 * (10000.0 + 100.0 * 100.0));
}
```

### Example 3: with a lognormal severity

Take the severity moments from the lognormal.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  const auto sev = actuarial::losses::lognormal_moments(7.0, 1.2);
  const auto m = actuarial::losses::compound_poisson(3.0, sev.mean.item<double>(), sev.variance.item<double>());
  out.writeln("mean =", m.mean.item<double>(), " variance =", m.variance.item<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: the same as the two steps

Equal to aggregate_moments on the Poisson moments.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(1));
  const auto n = actuarial::losses::poisson_moments(2.0);
  const auto a = actuarial::losses::aggregate_moments(n.mean, n.variance, pluss::make_series(100.0), pluss::make_series(10000.0));
  const auto b = actuarial::losses::compound_poisson(2.0, 100.0, 10000.0);
  out.writeln(a.mean.item<double>(), b.mean.item<double>(), a.variance.item<double>(), b.variance.item<double>());
}
```

### Example 5: the errors

A negative rate or severity moment is refused.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::losses::compound_poisson(-1.0, 100.0, 10000.0); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::losses::compound_poisson(2.0, -1.0, 10000.0); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::losses::compound_poisson(2.0, 100.0, -1.0); });
}
```

